<p>The U.S. State Department approved an “emergency” foreign military sale to Israel worth about $151.8 million. The package covers the transfer of 12,000 BLU-110A/B 1,000‑pound general-purpose bomb bodies (casings), plus related munitions support, and it bypasses the usual congressional review process. Secretary of State Marco Rubio cited an emergency determination as the legal basis for the waiver, according to the State Department account carried by Reuters and other outlets. Several outlets say the approval comes as the conflict involving Iran intensifies, and that the emergency determination is meant to speed delivery and support during what the U.S. describes as urgent security needs.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/3UgSwgc3oKpbar-S22P80KGgZVMTnZ4ssi8oUy0OSLCtuY0FNCwyJa_8FGv6xY707AgbY8w-dbIMgnxh7vQeYLfaOkHNJsICQ8Lc6_t0aKz7WlNdrE7Bu1KPVgxGTUb4WcAonmtbA7MYJrvXFS0S0fP0RHMn3pLaIeQILyfRfe1_pmKDUBhpz3qQbH6BLs7ZBFV6ar9FrQ295Qp6VYqNlhuFuC4_Q7BEDPNMzaS96mYe9YD8GTNrDVN7' alt='President Donald Trump speaks with Secretary of State Marco Rubio during a White House roundtable, March 6, 2026.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>What’s included: Along with the bomb bodies, the package includes U.S. government and contractor engineering, logistics, and technical support services, according to the State Department description.</li><li>Scale mismatch in shorthand: The State Department description lists BLU-110A/B bomb bodies, while a New York Times headline describes sending Israel “more than 20,000 bombs,” showing how different outlets characterize what’s being transferred.</li><li>Production ramp-up: Le Monde reports that major U.S. defense companies have agreed to quadruple production of advanced weapons, an effort aimed at boosting Israel’s ability to meet current and future threats.</li></ul><blockquote>This proposed sale will contribute to the foreign policy and national security of the United States by helping improve the security of a strategic regional partner that has been, and continues to be, an important force for political stability and economic progress in the Middle East, - U.S. Department of State</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>U.S. State Department: The department said the package supports U.S. foreign policy and national security by strengthening the security of a strategic regional partner, and cited an emergency need for immediate action. (<a href='https://www.al-monitor.com/originals/2026/03/us-skips-congressional-review-approve-munitions-sale-israel'>Al-Monitor (via Reuters)</a>)</li><li>Israel (requesting government): Israel requested BLU-110A/B general-purpose, 1,000-pound bomb bodies as part of the proposed transfer, the State Department said. (<a href='https://www.al-monitor.com/originals/2026/03/us-skips-congressional-review-approve-munitions-sale-israel'>Al-Monitor (via Reuters)</a>)</li><li>RT (reporting claim): RT highlights expert concerns about whether the allies have enough munitions for a prolonged campaign, and reports high civilian casualties in Iran during the strikes. (<a href='https://www.rt.com/news/634068-us-israel-bombs-sale/'>RT</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.ndtv.com/world-news/us-clears-emergency-sale-of-12-000-bomb-casings-worth-152-million-to-israel-middle-east-conflict-israel-us-iran-war-11180734#publisher=newsstand'>US Clears Emergency Sale Of 12,000 Bomb Casings Worth $152 Million To Israel</a> - ndtv.com</li><li><a href='https://www.thehindu.com/news/international/us-state-department-approves-emergency-weapons-sale-to-israel/article70714695.ece'>U.S. State Department approves ‘emergency’ weapons sale to Israel</a> - thehindu.com</li><li><a href='https://www.al-monitor.com/originals/2026/03/us-skips-congressional-review-approve-munitions-sale-israel'>US skips congressional review to approve munitions sale to Israel</a> - al-monitor.com</li><li><a href='https://www.nytimes.com/2026/03/06/us/politics/state-department-bomb-sale-israel.html'>Stat